---
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import MCQuiz from '../../components/MCQuiz.astro';
---

<Layout title="Why is the unauthorized user still hitting your action?" description="Practice card. A before_action that looks like it should block, but doesn't. Drills the halting rule.">
  <article class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 space-y-6">
    <div>
      <a href="/" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-6">
        <span>←</span> Back to Course
      </a>
      <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-ruby font-semibold mb-2">Practice · Reading the Source · Card 4</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-ruby-dark mb-4">Why is the unauthorized user still hitting your action?</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600">A <code class="bg-gray-100 px-2 py-1 rounded text-base">before_action</code> that reads like it should block non-admins. The action still runs. The bug is one missing line.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6 items-start">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
            <Icon name="lucide:search" class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-500 inline-block" aria-hidden="true" /> The code
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 italic mb-3">A non-admin sends <code>DELETE /posts/42</code>. The before_action runs, but the post still gets destroyed.</p>
          <div class="border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4 bg-gray-50/40">
            <CodeBlock code={`class PostsController < ApplicationController
  before_action :require_admin, only: [:destroy]

  def destroy
    @post = Post.find(params[:id])
    @post.destroy
    redirect_to posts_path
  end

  private

  def require_admin
    flash[:alert] = "Admins only" unless current_user.admin?
  end
end`} language="ruby" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">The question</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">What's the minimal change to <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">require_admin</code> that actually halts the callback chain?</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6 lg:sticky lg:top-6">
        <MCQuiz
          options={[
            {
              label: 'A',
              text: 'Return <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">false</code> from the callback.',
              explanation: 'Returning false halted the chain in Rails 4 and earlier. In Rails 5+ the return value of a before_action is ignored. This is the most common stale advice for this exact bug.',
            },
            {
              label: 'B',
              text: 'Call <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">render</code> or <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">redirect_to</code> when the user isn\'t admin.',
              explanation: 'Rails checks one thing between callbacks: has a response been set? <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">render</code> and <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">redirect_to</code> set it. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">flash[:alert] = "..."</code> mutates the flash without setting a response, so the chain keeps going.',
              correct: true,
            },
            {
              label: 'C',
              text: 'Raise an exception, e.g. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">raise "Forbidden"</code>.',
              explanation: 'Raising stops execution, but it stops it as a 500 error. Authorization isn\'t a server bug; it shouldn\'t look like one in your error tracker. Use a proper response.',
            },
            {
              label: 'D',
              text: 'Add <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">halt: true</code> to the <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">before_action</code> declaration.',
              explanation: 'That option doesn\'t exist in Rails. Halting is a runtime behavior based on whether the callback set a response, not a declaration option.',
            },
          ]}
          principle={`<p>The halting rule is one sentence: <strong>between callbacks, if a response has been set, halt the chain.</strong> Otherwise keep going.</p><p>Calling <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">render</code> or <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">redirect_to</code> sets a response. Mutating <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">flash</code>, setting instance variables, logging, or returning false does not. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">throw :abort</code> also halts, but you almost never need it because <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">render</code> / <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">redirect_to</code> cover the cases where you want the user to see something specific.</p><p>This is the most common authorization-bug shape in Rails: a callback that "looks" like it blocks but only mutates state.</p>`}
          theoryHref="/lessons/source-before-action"
          theoryLabel="Reading the Source · Card 6 — How before_action chains run"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex justify-between items-center pt-4">
      <a href="/lessons/practice-source-require-vs-bracket" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">← params.require vs params[]: what's the failure mode?</a>
      <a href="/lessons/practice-source-raw-form-missing" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">What's missing from this hand-written form? →</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
